  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on outcomes with probability less than 20%, adjusting their weights upward. However, the only potentially relevant outcome—the 0 Euro outcome in Lottery B—has exactly 20% probability, which does not meet the 'less than 20%' criterion. Therefore, strictly speaking, the reason does not apply any adjustment and would not favor Lottery B. The individual, however, explicitly chooses Lottery B because of the 'chance to lose nothing,' indicating sensitivity to the possibility of a favorable low-probability outcome. While this reflects a form of probability weighting or sensitivity to rare events, it does not align precisely with the formal rule of the decision reason, which excludes 20% from adjustment. The individual’s reasoning resembles a 'possibility effect'—giving disproportionate attention to the mere existence of a positive outcome—even if its probability is not below the defined threshold. Thus, while the individual’s thinking is in the spirit of the reason (attending to low-probability outcomes), it does not match the exact application of the rule. The information considered (presence of a no-loss chance) is partially aligned, but the rule-based preference (weighting only <20%) is not followed. Therefore, the match is partial but not exact.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
